             <p>ForkMesh stores bounded account records, encrypted private profile fields, repository catalog and blind-index metadata, generalized presence, pending verification records, and minimized operational/security logs. Room frames marked for persistence are retained for up to 7 days, with at most 500 replayable frames per room. Default room keys are derived and distributed by the relay, so its operator can decrypt that history; default rooms are not end-to-end encrypted. Private repository names reach the Worker on authenticated control-plane requests and are encrypted at rest under the instance data key. Private repository content and new private-agent payloads use separate client-side owner-sealed boundaries; platform administrators do not automatically receive those recipient private keys. An upgraded deployment cannot report its D1-backed API ready while any historical wallet key remains: the explicit offline process must first export recoverable keys, reconcile every public address at zero, scrub the key fields, and leave only key-free public reconciliation evidence.</p>
             <h2>Install diagnostics</h2>
             <p>The desktop installer reports anonymous, aggregate diagnostics about each install — which step it reached, whether that step succeeded, and coarse platform facts. Each run uses a fresh random id that is never linked to your account, email, node name, or IP address. Opt out by setting <code>FORKMESH_NO_DIAG=1</code> before running the installer.</p>
+            <h2>Referring websites</h2>
+            <p>When another website links a visitor here, ForkMesh increments a public per-hostname counter that powers the referring-site leaderboard on <a class="text-foreground underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-white" href="/referrals">/referrals</a>. Only the referring hostname and its running visit count are stored — never the referring URL’s path or query string, the page you landed on, your IP address, your user agent, or any per-visit record. If your browser sends no <code>Referer</code> header (links marked <code>rel="noreferrer"</code> do this for you), nothing is counted at all.</p>
             <h2>Documentation feedback</h2>
             <p>If you use a documentation helpfulness control, ForkMesh stores the like/dislike choice, a generalized <code>/docs/</code> section anchor, an optional message you deliberately submit, a broad client category, and a keyed network token used for abuse control. It does not retain the raw IP, browser user-agent, query string, search value, or a repository path in that record.</p>
             <h2>Wallets and community rewards</h2>
